                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_all:           `<strong>You named it as one system.</strong> Pricing speed, channel economics and data capability are not three problems — they compound. The quote engine needs the data platform, the platform reshapes the agents' role, and the agents decide whether any of it reaches the market. Now design against the whole, not one slice.`,
                    coach_r1_speed:         `<strong>Speed alone is a symptom, not the threat.</strong> Yes, 14 minutes versus three loses quotes — but a fast quote engine still runs on a data platform you don't have, sells through agents you haven't brought along, and prices with a model IVASS is watching. Fix only the clock and the other two will sink you.`,
                    coach_r1_channel:       `<strong>The channel matters, but it isn't the whole threat.</strong> The 78% tied book is your moat and your constraint — yet digitizing agents without a data platform or a faster quote leaves Lumo pricing in three minutes while you optimise paperwork. Diagnose the system, not one organ.`,
                    coach_r1_data:          `<strong>Data capability is necessary, not sufficient.</strong> A platform with no quote-speed gain and no agent plan is infrastructure nobody can sell through. The platform is the dependency the others sit on — but on its own it doesn't defend the book.`,
                    coach_r1_obstacle:      `<strong>Treating the rules as an obstacle bolted on at the end is the classic trap.</strong> In a regulated industry the EU AI Act gate is part of time-to-market, not a step after it. Design the strategy first and you'll design an option set the regulator can veto. Map the box before you design.`,
                    coach_r1_ignore:        `<strong>'IVASS rarely acts fast' is exactly the assumption the sector letter just demolished.</strong> They have put algorithmic pricing on notice; move-and-document-later on a high-risk AI use is the fastest route to a finding and a forced pause. The box is not a formality.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>That is the diagnosis the board needed.</strong> One interlocking threat AND the regulatory box as a design parameter — that is Brunetta's point: regulation shapes the opportunity set, so the viable path is the one that turns each gate into a design constraint rather than a brake. Build on that frame.`,
                    coach_r1_lowthesis:     `<strong>Without a single testable thesis the board can't hold you to anything.</strong> Five workstreams and no one-sentence claim about what must be true in three years is how transformation drifts. Commit to a thesis sharp enough to be wrong.`,
                    coach_r2_platform:      `<strong>Platform-first is durable — if you can survive the wait.</strong> Rebuilding the data and quote stack first is the lowest AI-Act exposure and the most defensible, but Lumo keeps pricing in three minutes while you build. Make sure the agents and a visible quick win carry you through the slow quarters.`,
                    coach_r2_channel:       `<strong>Channel-first protects the 78% book and the agents' goodwill</strong> — the network becomes an ally rather than a casualty. The risk is speed: while you digitize agencies, Lumo takes another two points. Pair it with at least a quote-speed gain so you're not purely defensive.`,
                    coach_r2_direct:        `<strong>A direct Lumo-killer is bold — and it just told 1,850 agencies you compete with them.</strong> 78% of EUR 3.1B runs through that network. If they slow-walk renewals for one quarter you lose more premium than the direct channel can win in three years. Where is your agent plan, and have you priced the AI-Act exposure?`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A balanced sequence is the path the system rewards.</strong> Data platform first, agents digitized in parallel, AI underwriting gated behind both — speed without abandoning the book, and every high-risk step has something to clear its gate against. Now hold the gating discipline.`,
                    coach_r2_noplatform:    `<strong>You starved the data platform — the dependency everything sits on.</strong> AI underwriting and a real-time quote both need it; underfund it and they strand. Fund the foundation before the floors built on top of it.`,
                    coach_r2_noagent:       `<strong>You barely funded the agents who carry 78% of your premium.</strong> Digitization that feels like 'cutting out the agents' invites an organized slowdown no technology can offset. The network can veto the strategy — fund the change, don't announce it.`,
                    coach_r2_upfront:       `<strong>You committed the full €90M in Year 1 with no milestone gates.</strong> When the data platform slips two quarters, the AI underwriting built on top of it is stranded and you've spent half the budget with nothing live. Why forgo the option to learn before you scale? Gate the tranches to milestones.`,
                    coach_r3_guardrails:    `<strong>Guardrails are the intuition-plus-analytics answer.</strong> Caps, a human-in-the-loop and a limited, bias-tested segment let you capture the model's upside while the underwriters' tail-risk judgment acts as a control — and it clears the EU AI Act obligation by design. You combined the two sources instead of choosing one.`,
                    coach_r3_modeled:       `<strong>You backed the model and overrode 25 years of loss experience.</strong> If it's over-fitted to a benign period, young-driver claims run hot exactly as the veterans warned and your combined ratio goes up, not down. Analytics without expert intuition as a control mis-prices the tail. What did those years know that your training data didn't?`,
                    coach_r3_reject:        `<strong>You sided with the veterans and shelved the model — safe, and a slow forfeit.</strong> Lumo prices in three minutes and just took another two points of your book. You protected this year's loss ratio and lost the next decade's relevance. The question was never veto-or-defer — it was how to use the underwriters to improve the model.`,
                    coach_r3_delay:         `<strong>Delaying for full bias testing is defensible — you cleared the gate.</strong> You miss the spring season, but you protected the firm from a finding and kept the model alive for a guardrailed launch next window. Just don't let 'delay' quietly become 'shelve'.`,
                    coach_r3_nooversight:   `<strong>No human oversight on a high-risk AI pricing use is a gate you didn't clear.</strong> The EU AI Act requires documented oversight and bias testing before deployment — not after. Put the underwriters in the loop as a live control, or the launch isn't compliant.`,
                    coach_r3_blindmodel:    `<strong>A wide, unguarded modeled launch is the tail-risk error.</strong> Eight months on, young-driver claims are running 30% above plan — the over-fit the underwriters warned about, magnified by how much of the book you exposed. Narrow it, put oversight in the loop, and let the data and the judgment discipline each other.`,
                    coach_r3_skipgate:      `<strong>You launched fast, wide and ungoverned to beat Lumo — and IVASS opened an inquiry.</strong> No bias testing, no documented oversight, citing the sector letter. You're looking at EUR 12–18M in remediation, a Solvency add-on, and you handed Lumo the spring season anyway. In a regulated industry the gate is part of time-to-market. Did speed buy you anything?`,
                    coach_r4_dependency:    `<strong>That sequence respects its own dependencies.</strong> Data platform before AI underwriting, agent buy-in before the channel shift — each step starts after what it needs, so nothing strands and the gates have something to clear against. This is sequencing governed by dependencies, not by which initiative is most exciting.`,
                    coach_r4_aifirst:       `<strong>You scheduled AI underwriting in Q2 but the platform it depends on lands in Q6.</strong> The sequence ignores its own dependencies — the AI build sits idle and stranded while the foundation is still under construction. What has to be true before each step can start?`,
                    coach_r4_channelfirst:  `<strong>You shifted the channel before retraining the agents.</strong> Pushing customers direct while the network is unprepared and unincentivized is how the 1,850 agencies slow-walk a quarter of renewals. Agent buy-in comes before the channel shift, not after.`,
                    coach_r4_postgate:      `<strong>Clearing the compliance gate after go-live is skipping it.</strong> Document-if-asked on a high-risk AI use invites the finding and the forced pause. The gate is part of time-to-market — clear it before go-live, every high-risk step.`,
                    coach_r4_noagent:       `<strong>No change plan for the network carrying 78% of your premium.</strong> 'The agents will adapt' is how a transformation becomes a casualty list. Incentives, retraining and a clear gain — sequenced before any channel shift — are what turn the agents from veto into ally.`,
                    coach_r4_boardready:    `<strong>That defends to the board and IVASS.</strong> A dependency-ordered sequence, every high-risk step gated before go-live, and a real agent change plan — compliance held, capital protected, agents engaged and momentum visible. That is leading change under uncertainty: turning every constraint into a design parameter.`,

